-- Level 1 deepening: Affiliate Marketing, and No-Code App Building.
-- Additive only, one new module of two lessons each. Both go from
-- 1 module / 2 lessons to 2 / 4.
--
-- AFFILIATE MARKETING explained what it is and how to pick a niche, then
-- stopped -- before the learner knew what they would actually build or
-- whether the business still works. It gains Ahrefs' site blueprint and,
-- deliberately, Ahrefs' 2026 piece on what Google's helpful-content and
-- spam updates did to affiliate sites. A course that teaches this model
-- without saying that is out of date, and the honest version is also more
-- useful: the sites that survived had first-hand experience, which a
-- Kenyan learner writing about local prices and conditions has by
-- definition and almost nobody else is writing.
--
-- The notes also answer the question nobody answers for learners here:
-- CHECK THE PAYOUT METHOD FIRST. Amazon Associates does not pay to Kenyan
-- bank accounts by default. Months of work for a balance that cannot be
-- withdrawn is the single most wasted effort in this field, and the
-- lesson says to confirm it from the programme's own terms page.
--
-- NO-CODE APP BUILDING asked what no-code is and compared tools, then
-- Level 2 built in specific apps. The concepts underneath -- a screen is
-- a tree of components, and an app needs a database -- are identical in
-- every tool and were never taught. FlutterFlow is the worked example
-- because FlutterFlow University teaches them properly; the notes name
-- the equivalent vocabulary in Glide, Adalo and Bubble so the ideas
-- travel.
--
-- This also partly offsets a known defect: the Level 2 Glide lessons use
-- Glide's older product, which the vendor has moved on from. The new
-- module is the durable part.
--
-- SOURCING NOTE: both channels were found by pointing the several-
-- channels-per-vendor lesson at adjacent categories -- Ahrefs publishes a
-- numbered affiliate course, not just SEO, and FlutterFlow runs a
-- separate "FlutterFlow University" teaching series.
--
-- REJECTED on duration, all from the same two channels: Ahrefs' [1.1]
-- (1:59), [1.2] (2:33) and [1.3] (1:56) -- a numbered course whose early
-- parts are all under the floor. Ahrefs' "Complete Affiliate Marketing
-- Course for Beginners" runs 1h48m, too long for a Level 1 lesson when
-- data costs money. FlutterFlow's "Tour of FlutterFlow" (10:18) clears
-- everything but duplicates Level 1's existing tool comparison.
--
-- Every id confirmed through the YouTube Data API for channel, exact
-- title, duration and embeddability. The four run 4:18 to 10:58.
--
-- All four new lessons carry objectives, notes, a practice activity and a
-- knowledge check, so the 438-lesson bare backlog does not grow.

-- ============ Affiliate Marketing ============
-- Auto-generated by scripts/curriculum/build-level-sql.mjs -- do not
-- hand-edit the lesson content below; regenerate from the source plan
-- JSON instead if something needs to change.

do $$
declare
  v_course_id uuid := '4dead527-2b53-4173-8bd9-e0b20707c738';
  v_level uuid;
  -- modules.order_number is unique per COURSE, not per level (a course's
  -- modules are numbered continuously across all its levels) -- so this
  -- can't just start at 1 for a level beyond the first. Computed here
  -- instead of hardcoded so this script works correctly regardless of how
  -- many modules the course already has.
  v_module_offset int;
  v_m1 uuid;
begin
  -- Appending to a level that already exists. Looked up rather than
  -- inserted, so this adds to the learner's current Level 1 instead of
  -- creating a duplicate one, and no existing module is re-parented.
  select id into v_level from public.levels
    where course_id = v_course_id and order_number = 1;

  if v_level is null then
    raise exception 'Level % not found for course % -- refusing to guess', 1, v_course_id;
  end if;

  -- The new modules must sit INSIDE this level, not at the end of the
  -- course. getOrderedLessons() builds the learner's lesson sequence from
  -- modules.order_number alone and ignores level, so a module appended at
  -- the course's max order would put beginner content after the final
  -- level's material. Insert straight after this level's last module and
  -- shift everything below it down instead.
  select coalesce(max(order_number), 0) into v_module_offset
    from public.modules where level_id = v_level;

  -- Two-phase shift: (course_id, order_number) is unique, and a single
  -- "+ N" update can trip that constraint mid-statement, so park the rows
  -- out of range first and bring them back afterwards.
  update public.modules set order_number = order_number + 1000
   where course_id = v_course_id and order_number > v_module_offset;

  insert into public.modules (course_id, level_id, title, description, order_number) values
    (v_course_id, v_level, 'What It Actually Takes, in 2026', 'Level 1 explained what affiliate marketing is and how to pick a niche, then stopped. This module covers the two things a learner needs before spending months on it: what you actually build, and the honest state of the business now that Google''s updates have wiped out a great many affiliate sites. Both from Ahrefs.', v_module_offset + 1)
  returning id into v_m1;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m1, 'The Blueprint: What You Are Actually Building', 'The concrete shape of an affiliate site — the pages, the structure and the order to build them in — so the plan stops being abstract.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=559bgY-8aV8', 1,
      array['Describe the page types an affiliate site needs and what each one is for', 'Order the build so the first pages published are the ones that can earn', 'Recognise why a pile of unstructured posts does not become a business'],
      'The useful shift here is from "I will write about my niche" to "I will build these specific pages". Affiliate sites that earn are structured: pages that answer a buying question, pages that compare options, and pages that catch people who are still learning. They link to each other on purpose.

Build order matters more than people expect. Publishing twenty general articles before a single page that could earn is the most common way a year disappears with no income. Start with the pages closest to a purchase decision, because those are the ones an affiliate link belongs on at all.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the money question nobody answers for Kenyan learners. Most large affiliate programmes pay by bank transfer, PayPal or cheque. Amazon Associates does not pay to Kenyan bank accounts by default and its threshold and methods vary by country, so CHECK THE PAYOUT METHOD BEFORE you build anything around a programme. This is the single most wasted effort in affiliate marketing here: months of work for a balance that cannot be withdrawn. Programmes that pay via PayPal, Wise or direct international transfer are the practical starting point, and you should confirm it in writing from the programme''s own terms page, not from a YouTube video.

Also worth planning for: a site takes months to earn anything. If you need money this quarter, the freelancing, virtual assistance or print-on-demand paths on this platform pay sooner. Affiliate marketing is a slow asset, and treating it as quick income is how people give up at month three.',
      'Sketch your site on one page: list the first five pages you would publish, and next to each write which one could carry an affiliate link and why someone reading it would be close to buying. Then pick one affiliate programme in your niche, find its terms page, and write down its payout methods, minimum threshold and whether it pays to Kenya. If you cannot find that in writing, that is your answer about the programme.',
      '[{"question":"Why does build order matter on an affiliate site?","options":["Search engines index older pages first","Publishing many general articles before any page close to a purchase decision is how a year passes with no income","Affiliate programmes require a minimum page count","Hosting costs rise with the number of pages"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What should you confirm about an affiliate programme before building around it?","options":["How many other affiliates it has","Whether its website looks professional","Its payout methods, threshold and whether it actually pays to Kenya — in writing, from its own terms","Whether it offers a mobile app"],"correct_index":2}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'What Google''s Updates Did, and What Still Works', 'An honest look at the affiliate business after the search updates that destroyed many affiliate sites — and what the ones that survived did differently.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sk8MAbD39Qw', 2,
      array['Explain why large numbers of affiliate sites lost their traffic', 'Identify what distinguished the sites that kept growing', 'Decide honestly whether this model suits what you can actually offer'],
      'This lesson exists because the alternative is selling you a dream. Google''s helpful-content and spam updates removed the traffic from an enormous number of affiliate sites, including ones built exactly as older tutorials taught. Any course that teaches affiliate marketing without saying so is out of date.

What the survivors had in common is not a trick. It is first-hand experience worth reading — actually using the product, actually knowing the field, showing something a summary of other people''s reviews cannot show. Sites that rewrote what was already online lost. Sites with something of their own kept going.

That is a demanding bar, and it is also an opportunity if you take it seriously: you know things about your market that an overseas affiliate writer does not. Prices in Nairobi shops, which models actually survive local conditions, what the warranty situation really is, which seller delivers. That is first-hand content by definition, and almost nobody is writing it.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — decide now, before the work. Ask yourself honestly: can I write something about this niche that is not already on the internet? If the answer is no, affiliate marketing will be a long, unpaid apprenticeship. If the answer is yes — you repair phones, you farm, you teach, you have used the thing — then you have the one asset the surviving sites have, and the rest of this course is worth your time.',
      'Write down three things you know about your chosen niche from direct experience that you could not have got from reading — a price you paid, a product that failed, a step everyone gets wrong. Then search for each one and see whether anyone has written it. What you cannot find is your actual advantage. If you could not fill all three lines, consider a different niche, one you have genuinely lived.',
      '[{"question":"What did the affiliate sites that kept growing have in common?","options":["They published more often than their competitors","First-hand experience worth reading — something a summary of other people''s reviews cannot show","They used paid advertising instead of search","They targeted less competitive countries"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Why is local, lived knowledge an advantage in affiliate content now?","options":["Search engines rank local domains higher","It is cheaper to produce than researched content","It is first-hand by definition — prices, failures and conditions an overseas writer cannot know — which is exactly what survived the updates","Affiliate programmes pay more for local traffic"],"correct_index":2}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

  -- Bring the shifted modules back, now sitting after the 1 new one(s).
  update public.modules set order_number = order_number - 1000 + 1
   where course_id = v_course_id and order_number > 1000;

end $$;

